On Thu, 18 Nov 2004, Jacobs wrote:

> What is the one change that would make the most difference to your success
> rate?
>
> And don't say play slower, because I think that's true for many of us.
> More particularly, what do you tend to do when you're playing too fast?

Let me start more positive and note the changes in my playing style which
/have/ improved my success rate. I am pretty sure that these are classical
newbie mistakes:

First, I tended to spare escape resources for emergency cases even in case
of an emergency, which meant that I frequently died with useful scrolls or
wands. Now it is not below me to blast a jackal with a wand of lightning
when he is blocking a vital escape route.

Also, in my heart I am a pack-rat. For example, I carried all items to an
altar even if it meant a trip of 15 levels to mine town (I might exchange
my +1 pair of iron shoes for a +2 pair), I collected gems and gold (they
give higher score), I even explored Gehennom fully (there might lie a
magic marker somewhere). But this boring, repetitive work makes me
careless and leads to stupid deaths; not to mention that lingering too
much on early levels is a disadvantage on its own.


Nowadays, I play pretty carefully after my characters have reached level
10 or so. Then, my main reason of death is planning too far ahead. (A
stupid death in the tournament occured when I was going to Rodney and was
focused on the forthcoming battle with him instead of paying attention to
my hunger status.) I should learn to keep my mind focused on the situation
at hand even it is just an intermediate step to a more interesting
encounter. Before this point, I die because of carelessness, mainly when
running into unexplored areas instead of resting and healing, or when
moving to fast and running into a floating eye (Yes, I am playing for >10
years and still die floating eye deaths.).


Anyway, when I started playing NetHack, I felt that real-life luck were
playing a dominant role; I have since learned that most of my characters
can go very far if played carefully.

> For instance, I would ascend twice as often if I'd would just take
> breaks whenever I get a very succesful character

This would be a detriment to my ascension rate. For me the time
immediately after restarting a game, where I still am a bit disoriented
and not quite in-game, is always a critical point.

